Sure, a "loop" is a way to repeatedly execute a set of statements.  In C, you can use three kinds of loops: "for", "while", and "do-while".  Here’s how each one works:

For:
```
for (initialization; condition; update) {
    // statements to be executed repeatedly
}
```

The "initialization" statement is run once at the start of the loop, the "condition" statement is checked each time the loop is run, and the "update" statement is run each time the loop is run.  Here’s an example:

For(int i=0; i<10; i++) {
    cout << i << endl;
}

The loop will run 10 times, and will execute each time the "i < 10" condition is true.  "i" is the loop variable, and each time it is incremented by 1.  The "endl" statement outputs a line of text to the console.

While:
```
while (condition) {
    // statements to be executed repeatedly
}
```

The "condition" statement is checked each time the loop is run.  If it is true, the "statements to be executed repeatedly" are executed, and then the "condition" is checked again.  Here’s an example:

while(count < 10) {
    cout << count << endl;
    count++;
}

The loop will run until "count < 10" is false, and will increment "count" by 1 each time the "condition" is true.  The "endl" statement outputs a line of text to the console.

Do-While:
```
do {
    // statements to be executed repeatedly
} while (condition);
```

The "condition" statement is checked each time the loop is run.  If it is true, the statements to be executed are run, and then the "condition" is checked again.  Here’s an example:

do {
    cout << "Hello World!" << endl;
} while (true);

The loop will run once, and then the "condition" is checked each time it is true.  If true, the "Hello World!" message is printed to the console.  The "true" constant is a Boolean value that is true as long as the condition is true.

